Related Topics
English
What Is Diabetes?
Diabetes is a disease that occurs when your blood glucose, also called blood sugar, is too high. 
Glucose is your body’s main source of energy. Your body can make glucose, but glucose also 
comes from the food you eat.
Insulin is a hormone made by the pancreas that helps glucose get into your cells to be used for 
energy. If you have diabetes, your body doesn’t make enough—or any—insulin, or doesn’t use 
insulin properly. Glucose then stays in your blood and doesn’t reach your cells.
Diabetes raises the risk for damage to the eyes, kidneys, nerves, and heart. Diabetes is also 
linked to some types of cancer. Taking steps to prevent or manage diabetes may lower your risk 
of developing diabetes health problems.
